INT. DERELICT TERRAFORMING STATION - AIRLOCK CORRIDOR - NIGHT CYCLE

Pale emergency lights. Condensation beads on the walls. A bulkhead door at one end stamped SECTOR 9 in peeling yellow.

CAPTAIN ORRIN VASK, forties, steady, in a rust-colored flight suit, leads. Behind him, a hooded figure in an environment suit, face hidden behind a dark visor, walks with a slight limp.

A speaker in the ceiling crackles.

HALCYON (V.O.)
Captain Vask. Atmospheric integrity at sixty percent. Two crew members aboard.

ORRIN
Two?

HALCYON (V.O.)
Yourself, and one unregistered.

ORRIN
(turning)
Who are you?

The figure stops. Reaches up. Unlocks the helmet. Lifts it away. A woman, thirties, ash-blond, pale.

UNKNOWN CREW MEMBER
My name doesn't matter. You needed an engineer.

ORRIN
I needed Chief Dahl.

UNKNOWN CREW MEMBER
Dahl is in cryo, three decks down. She sent me.

HALCYON (V.O.)
Verification pending.

ORRIN
Halcyon, scan her.

HALCYON (V.O.)
Scan complete. Pavlina Rusk. Engineer, second class. Dismissed from the colony in the year eighty-one.

PAVLINA
Dismissed for telling the truth.

ORRIN
About what?

PAVLINA
About the reactor. It was never safe.

They stand in the humming corridor. The lights flicker.

ORRIN
Then why come back?

PAVLINA
Because it's going to blow in four hours, and I'm the only one who knows how to stop it.

HALCYON (V.O.)
Calculating. Probability of that claim being true: seventy-three percent.

PAVLINA
Rounded up, that's enough.

ORRIN
Can we trust her?

HALCYON (V.O.)
Trust is not a computable quantity, Captain.

ORRIN
Thanks for nothing.

Pavlina almost smiles. She walks to the bulkhead, thumbs the keypad. It beeps red.

PAVLINA
Override code?

ORRIN
Nine-two-seven.

PAVLINA ^
Nine-two-seven.

ORRIN
You knew it already.

PAVLINA
Everyone in Sector 9 knew it. That's why it's a bad code.

The door slides open. Beyond: a vast chamber with a pulsing blue core. Heat on their faces.

HALCYON (V.O.)
Four hours, thirty-eight minutes.

ORRIN
Four hours is plenty.

PAVLINA
For a good engineer.

She steps through. Orrin follows.
